'''Jalube, S.A.''' is a Balboan {{w|specialty chemicals}} company specialising in {{w|lubricant|finished lubricants}} and {{w|synthetic oil|synthetic base stocks}}, with a secondary business in {{w|plasticizer}}s. Headquartered in Zone 2, [[Sant Bart]], the company is the largest domestic producer of finished lubricants in Balboa, supplying automotive, industrial, and marine markets. The company's name is a portmanteau of "Jala" — the province where [[CIEA]]'s [[Badia Curta]] refinery, the source of the company's base oil supply, is located — and "lube".

Jalube is listed on the [[Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor]] (SCX: JLB) and is a constituent of the [[SCX 100]] index. The company's plasticizer business, while smaller than that of [[Grup Rojo]], competes in the domestic adipate plasticizer market.

== History ==

=== Founding (1972) ===

Jalube was founded in 1972 by [[Jordi Serra i Pujol]], a chemical engineer from Sant Bart who had worked in the oil distribution sector. Serra identified an opportunity to produce finished lubricants domestically, using {{w|base oil}}s from [[CIEA]]'s (then Petrolí de Balboa's) [[Badia Curta]] refinery. At the time, most finished lubricants sold in Balboa were imported from international oil companies, and Serra believed that a domestic blender with a reliable base oil supply could compete on cost and service.

Serra secured a long-term base oil supply agreement with Petrolí de Balboa and built the company's first lubricant blending plant in Zone 2, Sant Bart, with backing from private investors in the interior provinces. The plant received base oils from Badia Curta by rail. The company was named "Jalube" — a combination of "Jala" (the province where the refinery is located) and "lube" (short for lubricant).

The early product range consisted of conventional automotive motor oils, gear oils, and industrial lubricants, blended from {{w|Group I base stock|Group I base oils}} supplied by CIEA. The company initially served the domestic market, focusing on the interior provinces where imported lubricants were expensive and supply was inconsistent.

=== Synthetic lubricants and expansion (1980s) ===

In the early 1980s, Jalube invested in {{w|synthetic oil|synthetic base stock}} production, building an {{w|esterification}} unit at its Sant Bart complex. The unit produced {{w|ester|synthetic ester}} base stocks — including {{w|adipate ester}}s and {{w|polyol ester}}s — for high-performance lubricant applications, including low-temperature automotive oils, aviation lubricants, and food-grade lubricants.

The establishment of the [[Règim de compensació a l'exportació|negative tariff]] in 1981 made Jalube's finished lubricant exports competitive, and the company began exporting to [[BTPMEU]] and Illypnian markets. The company also expanded its automotive product range, introducing semi-synthetic and full-synthetic motor oil lines.

=== Plasticizer diversification (1980s–1990s) ===

The esterification technology developed for synthetic lubricant base stocks also enabled Jalube to produce {{w|adipate}} {{w|plasticizer}}s — specifically {{w|dioctyl adipate}} (DOA) and {{w|diisodecyl adipate}} (DIDA) — which are chemically similar to the adipate esters used as synthetic lubricant base stocks. The company began producing adipate plasticizers in the late 1980s, primarily for the domestic PVC industry, where they are used in low-temperature and food-contact applications.

The plasticizer business remained a secondary diversification, leveraging existing technology and feedstocks rather than representing a strategic pivot. It accounted for approximately 15 per cent of revenue and brought Jalube into competition with [[Grup Rojo]], the dominant domestic plasticizer producer, which offers a broader range of both phthalate and non-phthalate plasticizers.

=== Listing and growth (1994–present) ===

Jalube was listed on the [[Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor]] in 1994. The listing raised capital that was used to expand the blending plant, upgrade the esterification unit, and build a dedicated marine lubricant facility at [[Port de Sant Cristòfor]] to serve vessels transiting the [[Balboa Canal]]. The [[Fons d'Inversió Directa de Balboa]] (FIDB) acquired a 5 per cent stake at the time of the listing.

Through the 2000s and 2010s, Jalube expanded its automotive lubricant range, introducing {{w|Group III base stock|Group III}} and {{w|PAO|polyalphaolefin}} (PAO) based products to meet the demands of modern engines. The company also developed a range of {{w|grease (lubricant)|greases}} — lithium, calcium, and polyurea-based — for industrial and automotive applications.

The establishment of the [[BTPMEU]] in 1998 opened the Tapuya and Potocsí markets for Jalube's products, and the company established distribution networks in both countries. Marine lubricants became a growing segment, driven by increasing [[Balboa Canal]] traffic and the expansion of the Balboan-flagged merchant fleet.

== Products ==

Jalube's product portfolio spans three main categories:

=== Finished lubricants ===

* '''Automotive lubricants''': Motor oils (passenger car, heavy-duty diesel), transmission fluids, and gear oils, in conventional, semi-synthetic, and full-synthetic formulations. The company's flagship product line is its full-synthetic PAO-based motor oil, positioned as a premium offering for the domestic and BTPMEU markets.
* '''Industrial lubricants''': Hydraulic fluids, turbine oils, compressor oils, and circulating oils for manufacturing, mining, and power generation. Industrial lubricants are the company's largest revenue category, driven by demand from Balboa's industrial sector.
* '''Marine lubricants''': System oils, cylinder oils, and trunk piston engine oils for vessels transiting the [[Balboa Canal]] and calling at Balboan ports. The marine lubricant business is based at the Port de Sant Cristòfor facility, which allows for direct delivery to vessels.
* '''Greases''': Lithium, calcium, and polyurea-based greases for automotive, industrial, and marine applications.

=== Synthetic base stocks ===

* '''Ester base stocks''': Synthetic ester base stocks (adipate esters, polyol esters) for high-performance lubricant formulations, produced at the company's esterification unit in Sant Bart. These are used both in Jalube's own finished lubricant products and sold to external lubricant blenders.
* '''PAO base stocks''': Polyalphaolefin base stocks, produced from ethylene (sourced from CIEA's Badia Curta steam cracker output), used in the company's full-synthetic product lines.

=== Plasticizers ===

* '''DOA (dioctyl adipate)''': A low-temperature plasticizer for PVC, used in food packaging, medical products, and outdoor applications. Produced from adipic acid and 2-ethylhexanol.
* '''DIDA (diisodecyl adipate)''': A higher-molecular-weight adipate plasticizer for improved permanence in PVC compounds.

The plasticizer business is a secondary diversification, accounting for approximately 15 per cent of revenue. Jalube competes with [[Grup Rojo]] in the adipate plasticizer segment, though Grup Rojo offers a broader range of both phthalate and non-phthalate plasticizers.

== Operations ==

* '''Headquarters''': 34 Carrer 4t, Zone 2, [[Sant Bart]]
* '''Sant Bart complex''': Lubricant blending plant, esterification unit, PAO unit, grease plant, and plasticizer unit. The complex receives base oils from CIEA's Badia Curta refinery by rail. Approximately 2,200 employees.
* '''Port de Sant Cristòfor facility''': Marine lubricant blending and delivery facility, serving vessels transiting the [[Balboa Canal]] and calling at the Port of Sant Cristòfor. Approximately 500 employees.
* '''Feedstocks''': Base oils from [[CIEA]]'s Badia Curta refinery; ethylene for PAO production from CIEA's steam cracker; adipic acid and oxo alcohols for esterification (oxo alcohols sourced from [[Grup Rojo]] and international suppliers)
* '''Employees''': approximately 3,000 (2026)

The Sant Bart complex is Jalube's primary production site, housing all of the company's production units except the marine lubricant facility. The complex's location in the interior provinces provides access to the industrial and agricultural markets of the interior, while rail connections to Badia Curta and Sant Cristòfor provide efficient logistics for both feedstock supply and product distribution.

== Ownership ==

Jalube is publicly traded on the [[Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor]]. The ownership structure reflects the company's history as a family-founded business with strategic institutional investors:

* '''[[Serra family]]''': 18% — descendants of founder Jordi Serra i Pujol; represented on the board
* '''[[Fons d'Inversió Directa de Balboa]] (FIDB)''': 5% — acquired at the time of the 1994 listing
* '''[[Balboa Pension Fund]] (BPF)''': 4% — institutional anchor investor
* '''Public float''': approximately 73% — held by institutional and retail investors

== Competition ==

In the lubricants market, Jalube is the largest domestic producer, but competes with imported finished lubricants from international oil companies, including [[CIEA]]'s own branded lubricant line. The company's competitive advantages include its domestic base oil supply (which avoids import costs), the negative tariff on exports, and its marine lubricant business, which benefits from proximity to the [[Balboa Canal]].

In the plasticizer market, Jalube is a smaller player, competing with [[Grup Rojo]], the dominant domestic producer. Jalube's plasticizer business is limited to adipate plasticizers, while Grup Rojo offers a full range of phthalate and non-phthalate plasticizers. The two companies also have a supplier relationship: Jalube purchases 2-ethylhexanol from Grup Rojo for use in both esterification (for synthetic base stocks) and plasticizer production.

